2.  When to Use FSM vs Simple Combinational Logic
When should FSM be used instead of simple combinational logic?
a.  Comparison:

| Criteria              | FSM    | Combinational Logic |
|-----------------------|--------|---------------------|
| State memory          | Yes    | No                  |
| Sequential processing | Yes    | No                  |
| Speed                 | Slower | Faster              |
| Resource              | More   | Less                |
| Complexity            | Higher | Lower               |

b. When to Use FSM:

| Situation              | Example                    |
|------------------------|----------------------------|
| ≥ 3 states             | Vending Machine (6 states) |
| Event ordering         | Coin → Select → Dispense   |
| Need state memory      | "Has enough money?"        |
| Sequential processing  | Step-by-step flow          |
| User interaction       | User interface             |

c.  When NOT to Use FSM:

| Situation         | Example         |
|-------------------|-----------------|
| 1-2 states        | LED on/off      |
| No state memory   | Simple addition |
| Simple processing | AND/OR logic    |
| High speed needed | Data path       |

d. In This Design:

| Component       | Uses FSM? | Reason                 |
|-----------------|-----------|------------------------|
| Vending Machine |  Yes      | 6 states, needs memory |
| Buzzer driver   |  Yes      | Counts beeps           |
| Debounce        |  No       | Simple filter          |
| Display         |  No       | Simple output          |

3.  Conclusion:
Use FSM when the system has **multiple states, event ordering, and needs state memory**. 
Use combinational logic for **simple, stateless operations**.
